      "id": 1,
      "text": "Newton Grove was first incorporated in 1879 as “New Town at the Grove” because of the prominent oak grove, then renamed Newton Grove in 1935.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newton_Grove%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Thirteen Oaks and the Isaac Williams House are listed on the National Register of Historic Places, highlighting Newton Grove’s rich architectural heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newton_Grove%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Hobbton High School, a small 1A division school founded in 1957, serves as the town’s primary public high school.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newton_Grove%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early records show the Newton Grove post office was once called Blackman’s Store, reflecting the influence of the Blackman family.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newton_Grove%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Blackman family, affluent planters who arrived in the early 18th century, were descendants of Jeremiah Blackman, a London mariner who helped bring English settlers to Virginia.",
